Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is “financial point of view” the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 9.3. Evaluation of Proposals. <omitted> (c) The Company Board, in evaluating any Acquisition Proposal, shall consider the following factors: (i) the financial terms of the proposal, including any amendments to financial terms, (ii) the strategic fit and long-term benefits to shareholders, (iii) the legal implications and potential liabilities as advised by the Company's outside legal counsel, (iv) the impact on the Company's reputation and ethical standing, and (v) compliance with fiduciary duties under applicable law. The Company Board, after consultation with both its outside legal counsel and a financial advisor of nationally recognized reputation, must determine in good faith that the Acquisition Proposal is a Superior Proposal, taking into account all relevant factors and the potential impact on the Company's stakeholders. The Company shall provide written notice to Parent, detailing the material terms and conditions of the Acquisition Proposal, and engage in good faith negotiations to address any concerns raised by Parent. Any material change to the terms of the Acquisition Proposal, including financial terms, shall require the Company to issue a new notice to Parent and restart the negotiation process. (Pages 102-104)\nAnswer:
